<!--
    decbench cases-missing pool: gnulib rpl_fcntl compiled at -O2 was the single
    hard per-function failure in 48 of 267 optimized x86-64 binaries (gzip,
    gnutls-serv, grep, tar, findutils, diffutils, 39 coreutils).  kuna emitted no
    body at all:

      rename_recurse: set_input_varnode (empty stack):
        Lowlevel { explain: "Overlapping input varnodes" }

    Upstream Ghidra raises the same LowlevelError on the same function (verified
    with Ghidra 12.1 headless on the gzip binary), so this is not a port defect;
    the fix is a kuna divergence, DIV-50 in docs/history.md.

    The bytes below are a reduced standalone build of the gnulib wrapper
    (gcc -O2 -fstack-protector-strong, rpl_fcntl at 0x11d0):

        int rpl_fcntl (int fd, int action, ...)
        {
          va_list arg; int result = -1;
          va_start (arg, action);
          switch (action) {
            case 0:    { int t = va_arg (arg, int); result = fcntl (fd, 0, t); break; }
            case 1030: { int t = va_arg (arg, int); ... F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC probe ... }
            default:   switch (action) {
                         case 1: case 3: ...:  result = fcntl (fd, action); break;
                         case 2: case 4: ...:  { int x = va_arg (arg, int);
                                                 result = fcntl (fd, action, x); break; }
                         default:              { void *p = va_arg (arg, void *);
                                                 result = fcntl (fd, action, p); break; }
                       }
          }
          return result;
        }

    Root cause, P3 dataflow.  gcc -O2 spills the register arguments into a
    va_list register-save area and reads the third argument back through an
    indexed load:

      11dd: mov %rdx,0x40(%rsp)          ; stack -0x38 = the third vararg
      123d: mov 0x10(%rsp),%eax          ; gp_offset
      124a: add 0x20(%rsp),%rax          ; + reg_save_area
      124f: mov (%rax),%rdx              ; -> resolves to stack -0x38 later

    The spill has no direct reader on the first stack heritage pass, so
    ActionDeadCode removes it; the indexed load only folds to the constant stack
    offset on a later pass.  From there heritage invents an input for the
    now-undefined slot, SubvariableFlow narrows it to 4 bytes, and
    Heritage::guardInput tiles the 8-byte range with two write-masked 4-byte
    input pieces plus a concatenating PIECE.  The simplification pools then fold
    that PIECE away.  On the next pass a fresh free 8-byte read of the same range
    finds an empty variable stack, asks setInputVarnode for a full-size input,
    and collides with the leftover pieces.

    Stage model: P3 dataflow, SSA renaming (heritage.rs rename_recurse ->
    kuna_inputtile::new_tiled_input).  The leftover write-masked pieces are the
    value the read wants, so the collision is reconciled with the upstream
    Funcdata::combineInputVarnodes primitive instead of aborting.

    No option: the function produced no output at all before, and the new path
    runs only where setInputVarnode was about to raise; any other overlap still
    raises.  Single pass asserts the recovered body; pre-fix all 8 assertions
    fail, because the emitted shell is a bare warning comment.

    Both code chunks carry 256 trailing 0xc3 bytes so the flow follower stops at
    a RET instead of running off the end of the mapped image.
-->
